  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThere is a lot of uncertainty regarding temperatures and \nprecipitation over the next two days. Temperatures will, in part, \nride on cloud cover, and models have been struggling with the \ncurrent stratus deck east of the Missouri River. \n\nAn upper trough/low dips into ND Thursday as almost a cut-off low \nswings into the central and southern plains. At the sfc, a weak low \npressure system moves into the central plains with an H7 low farther \nnorth over Nebraska somewhat embedded and stacked in the cut-off \ntrough. This is farther south than previous runs and may keep any \nrain south of the Hwy 212 corridor but definitely south of Hwy 12. \nAs the sfc low moves in MO on Thursday, drier mid level air pushes \nin from the northwest across SD on the leading edge of a sfc high. \nThis drier air coupled with the troughing aloft will usher in caa \nand cause northwest winds to gust 35 to 50 mph across the area in \nthe afternoon and evening. A wind advisory has been issued for \nportions of central and north central SD. For now, have it ending at \n5z Friday, but may need a headline farther east for the remainder of \nthe night given the 45 to 50 kt winds at 850mb. The Coteau, at \nleast, could remain very gusty through the early morning hours given \nthe pressure gradient in the east and the 90 kt jet. Given the \nstrength of the winds, unsure how low temps will fall Friday morning \nas H85 temps fall into the negative single digits. The caa and the \nwinds could cancel each other out, or we may end up needing some \nfreeze headlines. With the winds on Thursday and min RH around 35 to \n45 percent across central SD, may also need to consider impact fire \nheadlines where fuels are burnable. \n\nHighs pressure builds in on Friday with a ridge transitioning over \nthe Rockies and Plains on Saturday. Above normal temperatures and \ndry conditions are expected Saturday through the first half of next \nweek.",
